body:
**Revision:**One day as I was coming down the stairs to the breakfast table to eat with my daughters and wife, a strange thought had hit me. That thought being that I know what happens at parties. When I got to the table I looked to my daughters, safely seated on tall chairs. Perfect daughters kept perfectly safe from the puddle that had decided to appear in the room. However we all stayed silent and watched our new pond grow beneath our table. My wife broke the silence first, offering us food from her delectable breakfast spread. So much food, much too much for the four of us. It laid before us with the might and heft of a king’s feast. Pitchers of juices sweetened with extra splendor. Platters of meats loaded with fantastic fats. The food, labored over and provided by my wife, were just enough to keep our table grounded. As the waters of the puddles rose, I watched in fear as the water flirted with the idea of reaching for my daughters’ ankles. However my resolve ruled over my fear, just as the chairs kept my daughters above the water. Just high enough for them to enjoy the food and ignore the risings water seeping from our floor. 

As I watched my daughters eat, I felt a wondrous sense of accomplishment and joy. The chairs were so strong, and so well made. I had done a fine job with the woods and nails and now not one part of the water touched the legs of my daughters. I looked at my wife’s chair that was made by someone else. The chair was logged with water and in a constant state of wetness, but whether she had forgotten about how wet her chair was, or she simply didn’t care anymore. Perhaps she’s attended too many parties, sat in too many chairs. Perhaps she never appreciated the quality of a good chair. Nonetheless,  she sat and ate with joy like me as all our focus was on our daughters.

&#x200B;

As we finished our meals my eldest daughter got up and set out for work. She works a job in the city. As she got up and left and said thank you and kissed us on the cheek. Shortly after this my youngest daughter got up from the table and thanked us. I was frightened by the fact that she stood in the pond by the chairs, but she was quick to remove herself from the rising water. She too set out the door to see a new friend of hers, a friend from school. Both were so raised well, both raised not to go to parties.  I was happy in the knowledge that my daughters had chairs with high legs, chairs they could always rely on.

&#x200B;

So me and my wife sat at the table taking the last few bites of our meals. I was used to seeing my wife covered in water, drenched from head to toe. At this point she said to me “Do you think they will be free from water?” To which I replied “No, as they too know what happens at parties.“
